1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,000
Hi, in this video we're going to take a look at the tools included with Burp Suite that allow you to work with opaque data that isn't human readable.

2
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:21,000
You can use these whilst analysing the attack surface to review the information being transmitted and understand more about how the application works.

3
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:31,000
You might find that some of the opaque data contains information such as a username or a timestamp and you can test whether editing the opaque data impacts the response.

4
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:36,000
You can use the inspector and decoder within Burp to decode and work with opaque data.

5
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:43,000
To see how this works, we're going to use the Modifying Serialized Data Types lab in our Web Security Academy.

6
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:46,000
We'll take a look at the inspector first.

7
00:00:46,000 --> 00:01:01,000
Once you've launched the lab, use Burp's Embedded Browser to log in and then go to Burp's Proxy HTTP History tab to identify a message that includes opaque data.

8
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:05,000
In the Proxy History tab, highlight the data you want to study.

9
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:09,000
The selected text will be shown in the inspector panel.

10
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:16,000
If Burp recognises an encoding format, it will automatically decode the data and display it in the inspector.

11
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:25,000
If the data has been decoded in the inspector panel, you can modify the decoded data in the panel to see how this impacts the response.

12
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:30,000
To test this out, right-click the request and select Send to Repeater.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:32,000
Go to the Repeater tab.

14
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Highlight the data and edit it in the inspector panel as described in the lab solution.

15
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:55,000
We're going to update the length of the username attribute to 13, change the username to Administrator, and change the access token to Integer 0.

16
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:01,000
Click on Apply Changes, and send the request.

17
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:05,000
You can now review the response to determine how the modification impacted the response.

18
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:11,000
In this case, we'll get a redirect which takes us to a page with access to the admin panel.

